        "id": 1341996
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-10-23T21:09:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The interrupt handler wants to determine if the most recently measured level on the signal is identical to what was recorded in the last execution of the interrupt handler, because if so, there must have been (at least) two edges since last time.\n\nIf you recall, I used to store a boolean value in `uint8_t head_level`, and do the comparison like this `!!current_level \u003d\u003d !!slot-\u003ehead_level`, to see if both were zero or both were non-zero.  Now I am working directly with gpio register masks for efficiency, it dawned on me that we could save a few cycles by not eliminating the need for the compiler to consider any two non-zero values \"equal\".  The new convention of the `uint32_t head_level` is that it is either zero or the value of `gpio_mask`, that is, it has the particular bit set, which this GPIO occupies in its value register.  This way, the interrupt handler will read a GPIO register, do logical-and with the gpio mask, and then do plain integer comparison against `head_level`.\n\nI have added a comment on the `head_level` field, briefly explaining the above.",
      "parentUuid": "de0a6801_a454ae49",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 487,
        "startChar": 21,
        "endLine": 487,
        "endChar": 69
      },
